    "result_text": "The only test class (`AcceptanceTest`, the others are a base and a helper) passes. The build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `timezone` field on the owner read model, derived from the owner's locality/region via the fixed region-to-timezone table.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `timezone` string property to the `Owner` schema (right after `locality`), documenting the fixed mapping NSW\u2192Australia/Sydney, VIC\u2192Australia/Melbourne, QLD\u2192Australia/Brisbane. The `OwnerDto` is generated from this spec at build time, so this adds the `timezone` getter/setter.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Added:\n   - A `@Mapping(target = \"timezone\", expression = \"java(toTimezone(owner))\")` on `toOwnerDto`.\n   - A `REGION_TIMEZONE` table (NSW/VIC/QLD \u2192 IANA names).\n   - A `toTimezone(Owner)` method that looks up the timezone from the owner's already-derived locality (`toLocality(owner)`), returning `null` when the region isn't in the table (e.g. `UNKNOWN`), so the field is simply absent then.\n\nI reused the existing `toLocality(owner)` derivation so `timezone` stays consistent with `locality` (both trace back to the region baked into the customer code / postcode), rather than recomputing the region independently.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreReturnsTimezoneForRegion` (Sydney/postcode 2000 \u2192 `locality` NSW, `timezone` Australia/Sydney) passes, and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test run, 0 failures).",
